# Cross-Agent Hot Reload Watcher (`cross-agent-hot-reload-watcher`)

Restarting a game engine every time an agent modifies an LTX configuration, Lua script, shader, or texture takes 30 to 90 seconds per test cycle. When Gemini 4 Argon and parallel Gemini Flash subagents (`argon-dynamic-subagent-swarm`) make rapid incremental changes, restarting wastes substantial time and compute.

This skill integrates Linux kernel `inotifywait` (and Windows `FileSystemWatcher`) filesystem event monitoring with engine hot-reload hooks to inject modified assets on-the-fly.

---

## 1. Hot Reload Architecture

```
[ Argon Orchestrator ] --+
                         |---> inotifywait (Event: modify/create) ---> Engine IPC / Socket / Signal
[ Flash Subagents ] -----+                                            - Reload Lua VM
                                                                      - Flush LTX section cache
                                                                      - Invalidate shader cache
```

---

## 2. Real-Time Watcher Daemon Script

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Watch target gamedata directories for modifications
inotifywait -m -r -e close_write --format '%w%f' \
  "${REPO_ROOT}/gamedata/scripts/" \
  "${REPO_ROOT}/gamedata/config/" \
  "${REPO_ROOT}/gamedata/shaders/" | while read -r modified_file; do

    echo "[*] Detected file change: ${modified_file}"

    # Hot reload Lua scripts if engine supports IPC or named pipe
    if [[ "${modified_file}" == *.script || "${modified_file}" == *.lua ]]; then
        echo "reload_scripts" > /tmp/game_engine_cmd_pipe 2>/dev/null || true
    fi

    # Hot reload textures / shaders
    if [[ "${modified_file}" == *.hlsl || "${modified_file}" == *.spv ]]; then
        echo "reload_shaders" > /tmp/game_engine_cmd_pipe 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
done
```

---

## 3. Benefits to Multi-Agent Velocity

1. **Instant Feedback:** Test assertions can observe new gameplay parameters in the live game window without dropping framerate or closing Wine/Proton.
2. **Zero Context Loss:** Running game state (player position, active level coordinates, memory allocations) remains intact across script edits.

## Windows 10/11 Native Counterparts (`windows-linux-cross-os-runtime-bridge`)
- **Packages & Toolchains:** Use `winget` / `scoop` / `choco` and `vswhere.exe` (MSVC / Clang-CL) on Windows in place of `pacman` / `yay`. For desktop packaging on Windows, target MSIX / WiX / WinGet manifests (`windows-app-engineering`).
- **Profiling & Time-Travel Debugging:** On Windows 10/11, use **Windows Performance Recorder (`wpr -start GeneralProfile` / `wpa.exe`)** in place of Linux `perf`, and **WinDbg Preview Time-Travel Debugging (`ttd.exe -launch <app.exe>`)** in place of Mozilla `rr`.
- **Hardware, Thermal & Power Profiles:** Query sensors via WMI/CIM (`Get-CimInstance -Namespace root/wmi MSAcpi_ThermalZoneTemperature`, `nvidia-smi`) and switch power plans via `powercfg /setactive` on Windows.
- **File System Hot-Reload & ADB:** Use `.NET System.IO.FileSystemWatcher` (`Register-ObjectEvent`) on Windows in place of `inotifywait`, and resolve `adb.exe` from `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Android\Sdk\platform-tools\adb.exe`.
